WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.712
Hi, I'm Adarsh.

00:00:01.712 --> 00:00:04.064
If you are on my portfolio, you must have

00:00:04.064 --> 00:00:08.474
an important question in your mind: Is it worth my time?

00:00:08.527 --> 00:00:11.545
Give me two minutes to help you decide.

00:00:11.545 --> 00:00:15.897
I'm a full-stack developer specializing in React, Node.js,

00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:16.945
and TypeScript.

00:00:16.945 --> 00:00:19.500
From the moment someone clicks on a button

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.085
to what happens behind the scenes on the servers,

00:00:22.085 --> 00:00:26.530
I want to understand every single thing in between.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:29.161
So let me give you a real example of my work.

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:32.327
I built a storage platform called Vault.

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:35.230
In the first version, every file passed through my server

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.503
and landed on my laptop.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:42.900
But a laptop can be turned off, and that's a big problem.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.661
When it's off, no one can upload any files.

00:00:46.661 --> 00:00:50.476
And our server had to carry every single file.

00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:55.179
I solved both problems by using a cloud storage provider

00:00:55.179 --> 00:00:56.973
and pre-signed URLs.

00:00:56.973 --> 00:01:01.148
Now users can directly upload their files to that URL.

00:01:01.148 --> 00:01:05.024
Now your file goes from your browser to storage.

00:01:05.024 --> 00:01:07.567
It never carries the file.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:12.891
I designed and built Vault myself while working at ProCodrr,

00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:18.288
with feedback from my mentor, Anurag, the founder of ProCodrr.

00:01:18.288 --> 00:01:21.579
I've also built booking flows at Harigurus.

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:25.570
And I used to write practice exercises for ProCodrr's courses,

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.715
answer students' doubts, and fix unexpected problems

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:31.942
and errors they ran into.

00:01:31.942 --> 00:01:35.715
Getting involved in production changed the way I work.

00:01:35.715 --> 00:01:39.494
Before, I called a feature done when I finished developing it.

00:01:39.494 --> 00:01:43.558
Now, before I call it done, I ask myself four questions:

00:01:43.558 --> 00:01:44.967
How do I test it?

00:01:44.967 --> 00:01:46.403
What could go wrong?

00:01:46.403 --> 00:01:48.294
What is the cost of running it?

00:01:48.294 --> 00:01:51.982
What needs to be in place before people can rely on it?

00:01:51.982 --> 00:01:54.788
Want to know more about the problems I faced

00:01:54.788 --> 00:01:58.764
while building this, and my approach to how I solved them,

00:01:58.764 --> 00:02:00.052
step by step?

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:02.794
Open the Vault case study next.

00:02:02.794 --> 00:02:05.570
The biggest lesson I've learned over the years

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.542
is that making a feature work is just the beginning.

00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:13.082
What truly matters is understanding what you're building,

00:02:13.082 --> 00:02:16.833
who you are building it for, the impact it creates,

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:20.959
and taking ownership of your work beyond just writing code.

00:02:21.058 --> 00:02:24.103
If that sounds like someone your team needs,

00:02:24.103 --> 00:02:25.852
and I meet your requirements,

00:02:25.852 --> 00:02:27.947
I would love to have a conversation.

